WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:01.920
Part 3.

2
00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:09.820
You will hear two students called Rosie and Colin discussing their Human Geography assignment.

3
00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:15.860
First, you have some time to look at questions 21 to 25.

4
00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:46.110
Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 25.

5
00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:54.390
<v ROSIE> Colin, I'm really struggling to think of a topic for our Human Geography assignment.

6
00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:55.550
<v COLIN> Me too, Rosie.

7
00:00:56.010 --> 00:01:04.170
<v COLIN> I'll tell you what, let's think about the different aspects of Human Geography and see if we can narrow the topic down a bit to help us decide.

8
00:01:04.489 --> 00:01:04.790
<v ROSIE> OK.

9
00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:06.890
<v ROSIE> So one aspect is population.

10
00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:10.850
<v ROSIE> That would be all about population density and migration and so on.

11
00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:12.930
<v ROSIE> Lots of facts and statistics.

12
00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:14.270
<v ROSIE> Maybe a bit boring?

13
00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:17.950
<v COLIN> Yeah, but quite straightforward to find on the internet.

14
00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:19.070
<v ROSIE> Suppose so.

15
00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:20.730
<v ROSIE> How about health?

16
00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:26.510
<v ROSIE> I'd never thought about the links between that and geography until Professor Lee gave us that lecture on cholera.

17
00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:33.950
<v ROSIE> How in the 19th century, a physician used street plans and plans of water supplies to find the source of a cholera epidemic.

18
00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:36.190
<v COLIN> Yes, fascinating, wasn't it?

19
00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:40.530
<v COLIN> Or we could do something more general, like economies.

20
00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:44.570
<v ROSIE> So how financial and commercial factors are linked to the physical environment?

21
00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:45.150
<v COLIN> Yeah.

22
00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:47.890
<v ROSIE> I thought that had been taken off the syllabus for this year.

23
00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:48.710
<v COLIN> Has it?

24
00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:51.650
<v ROSIE> I'm not sure, but it might be best to avoid it.

25
00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:52.250
<v COLIN> OK.

26
00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:55.470
<v COLIN> Maybe we could do something on culture.

27
00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:59.810
<v COLIN> We had that lecture about culture and geography last week.

28
00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:01.410
<v ROSIE> I didn't get much out of that.

29
00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:05.570
<v ROSIE> It was all so general and the lecturer didn't give any useful examples.

30
00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:08.110
<v COLIN> Yeah, I hardly took any notes.

31
00:02:08.389 --> 00:02:09.530
<v COLIN> It didn't seem worth it.

32
00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:10.230
<v ROSIE> Me neither.

33
00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:12.330
<v COLIN> We could focus on poverty.

34
00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:14.930
<v COLIN> That's something that's a global problem.

35
00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:20.250
<v ROSIE> The trouble is, Dr Lee was saying, that you have to be careful with some of the figures relating to poverty.

36
00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:22.530
<v ROSIE> They're sometimes deliberately manipulated.

37
00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:26.470
<v COLIN> You mean the information gets changed for political reasons?

38
00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:27.990
<v ROSIE> That sort of thing, yes.

39
00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:37.970
Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 26 to 30.

40
00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:16.940
Now listen and answer questions 26 to 30.

41
00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:22.380
<v ROSIE> So what are we going to do our assignment on?

42
00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:23.840
<v ROSIE> I'll tell you what.

43
00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.540
<v ROSIE> One of the possibilities we haven't discussed is urbanisation.

44
00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:31.000
<v ROSIE> And now over half the world's population lives in cities.

45
00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:32.400
<v ROSIE> That's really important.

46
00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:33.420
<v COLIN> OK.

47
00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:34.480
<v COLIN> Good idea, Rosie.

48
00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:35.700
<v COLIN> Let's do that.

49
00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:38.820
<v ROSIE> I love living in a big city, but of course there are problems.

50
00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:41.040
<v COLIN> Things like theft and robbery?

51
00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:48.060
<v ROSIE> Yes, but where I come from, that's linked to another more serious issue, which is that a lot of people don't have jobs.

52
00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:50.180
<v ROSIE> That's getting worse and worse.

53
00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:55.600
<v ROSIE> And we also still have quite a lot of people who are homeless, though that's not quite so bad as it was.

54
00:03:56.020 --> 00:04:03.000
<v COLIN> It'd be nice to talk about some positive developments, like some of the new developments on the outskirts of cities.

55
00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:03.700
<v ROSIE> Yeah.

56
00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:06.880
<v ROSIE> They've opened some massive new shopping centres outside my city.

57
00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:12.840
<v COLIN> Yeah, the same with mine, but it's meant a lot of the shops in the city centre are closing down.

58
00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:18.540
<v COLIN> But the outskirts are ideal for buildings that need a lot of space, like for conferences.

59
00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:20.760
<v COLIN> They've opened a couple of big ones.

60
00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:22.460
<v ROSIE> Yes, we've got some too.

61
00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:28.640
<v ROSIE> It'd be nicer to have more facilities for things like football too, but that's not happening where I live.

62
00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:29.820
<v COLIN> Same in my area.

63
00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:34.080
<v ROSIE> We could include something about developing disused industrial sites.

64
00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:38.840
<v ROSIE> It seems like a good idea because you're not doing any harm to the natural environment.

65
00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:42.740
<v COLIN> Yeah, but aren't the buildings architecturally significant?

66
00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:43.940
<v ROSIE> Not really.

67
00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:51.640
<v ROSIE> And what people forget is that they often used quite dangerous materials, chemicals and things, which haven't been properly cleared away.

68
00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.100
<v ROSIE> So the whole site has to be made safe.

69
00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.020
<v COLIN> That can't be cheap.

70
00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:58.200
<v COLIN> And I bet it's often not budgeted for.

71
00:04:58.440 --> 00:04:58.980
<v ROSIE> You're right.

72
00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:01.400
<v COLIN> Have you read about Masdar City?

73
00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:02.440
<v ROSIE> In Abu Dhabi?

74
00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:06.160
<v ROSIE> Yes, it was designed to be a green city, wasn't it?

75
00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:11.500
<v ROSIE> That might be a good example of a city which set out to depend entirely on renewable energy.

76
00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:14.260
<v COLIN> Yes, we should say something about that.

77
00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:18.760
<v COLIN> It was designed to be totally pedestrianised too, wasn't it?

78
00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:20.720
<v COLIN> With the transport underground.

79
00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:26.000
<v ROSIE> Yes, and they had big plans for recycling to reduce waste to the lowest possible level.

80
00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:28.680
<v ROSIE> But let's stick to talking about power sources.

81
00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:33.240
<v COLIN> Then there's that ecotown in England, Greenhill Abbotts.

82
00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:38.600
<v COLIN> It set out to conform to the usual principles, sustainability and so on.

83
00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:41.540
<v COLIN> A lot of people were against it at first.

84
00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:43.580
<v COLIN> They said the plans were unrealistic.

85
00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:47.140
<v COLIN> I'm not sure how far they've got with it.

86
00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:48.300
<v COLIN> I'll check.

87
00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:48.960
<v ROSIE> Right.

88
00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:51.400
<v ROSIE> So it looks as if we have a sort of plan.

89
00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:57.480
That is the end of Part 3.

90
00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:02.360
You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to Part 3.
